<!-- FEATURES -->
<section class="lib-section" aria-labelledby="features-title">
  <div class="lib-wrap">
    <div class="section-label">Features</div>
    <h2 id="features-title">What it does</h2>
    <div class="lib-features-grid" role="list">

      <div class="lib-feature-card" role="listitem">
        <h3>Session-Start Catalog</h3>
        <p>At session start the server hands the agent a catalog of installed skills, so it can search for a relevant one on its own. Whether it actually does depends on the model and client.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="lib-feature-card" role="listitem">
        <h3>Web Research</h3>
        <p>When no skill matches a topic, the server can search the web, rank sources (official docs, RFCs, style guides first), and return the text for the agent to write a new skill from. The result is only as good as the sources and the model, so read what it saves.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="lib-feature-card" role="listitem">
        <h3>Quality-Weighted Search</h3>
        <p>BM25 relevance blended with a heuristic quality score &mdash; specificity, examples, actionability, source authority. Indexing runs at sub-section level, so results point at a section rather than a whole file. The quality score is a heuristic and will sometimes rank badly.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="lib-feature-card" role="listitem">
        <h3>{{ versions.librarian.packs }} Community Packs</h3>
        <p>Browse and install packs without leaving your editor. Security, API design, Git, TypeScript, Docker, PostgreSQL, testing, and more. Contributions are screened for prompt-injection patterns in CI before merge, which catches known patterns rather than everything.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="lib-feature-card" role="listitem">
        <h3>Ed25519 Integrity</h3>
        <p>Skills are signed on write &mdash; including ones the agent creates at runtime &mdash; and the signature is checked on load. A file that no longer matches its signature is reported as <code>TAMPERED</code> instead of being served. This detects changes to skill files; it is not a defense against someone who can also use your signing key.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="lib-feature-card" role="listitem">
        <h3>Checked Network Paths</h3>
        <p>Fetches reject private, loopback, and cloud-metadata addresses, pin resolved IPs against DNS rebinding, and re-check each redirect hop. <code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code> is sent only to GitHub hosts. Fetched web content is wrapped in untrusted-data delimiters before the agent sees it.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="lib-feature-card" role="listitem">
        <h3>Transactional Installs</h3>
        <p>Pack installs are transactional &mdash; a mid-install failure rolls back rather than leaving your existing skills half-overwritten. Progressive disclosure reads through to disk, so the server does not depend on an in-memory cache surviving.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="lib-feature-card" role="listitem">
        <h3>Zero Dependencies</h3>
        <p>Built on Node.js built-ins only. Nothing from npm to install or audit. Web research uses the native HTTPS module. Cold start is quick and the install is small.</p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- TOOLS REFERENCE -->
<section class="lib-section" aria-labelledby="tools-title">
  <div class="lib-wrap">
    <div class="section-label">Tools Reference</div>
    <h2 id="tools-title">14 MCP tools exposed to your agent</h2>
    <div class="lib-table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="MCP tools reference table">
      <table class="lib-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Tool</th>
            <th scope="col">Description</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>find_and_load</td>
            <td>Search and return the top matching skill in one call. If nothing matches, researches the topic from ranked web sources and returns content for skill creation. The usual entry point.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>find_skill</td>
            <td>BM25 full-text search across skill names, descriptions, categories, and content. Returns ranked results with scores.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>load_skill</td>
            <td>Load a complete skill by name. Returns content and available section slugs for <code>load_section</code>.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>load_section</td>
            <td>Load a specific section within a skill by slug path for focused, minimal context loading.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>list_skills</td>
            <td>List all installed skills with metadata, categories, integrity status, and section slugs.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>browse_packs</td>
            <td>Browse available community skill packs with descriptions and install commands. Searchable by keyword.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>install_pack</td>
            <td>Download, validate, and install a skill pack from the community repo or a direct URL. Deduplicates and re-indexes.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>research_topic</td>
            <td>Search the web, score sources by authority (official docs first), and fetch content from the top results. Source ranking is a heuristic, so check what comes back.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>fetch_page</td>
            <td>Fetch a single URL and extract readable text. Strips HTML, preserves code blocks and headings.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>create_skill</td>
            <td>Validate, save, and index a new skill. Updates the catalog so the skill is searchable in the same session.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>validate_skill</td>
            <td>Check a skill's structure, frontmatter, and content guard compliance without saving.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>export_pack</td>
            <td>Write selected skills to disk as a shareable pack with <code>pack.json</code> manifest.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>skill_status</td>
            <td>Check cryptographic integrity of a single skill. Reports VERIFIED, TAMPERED, or UNSIGNED.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>server_status</td>
            <td>Returns version, skill count, uptime, index stats, and configuration summary.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- SKILL FORMAT -->
<section class="lib-section" aria-labelledby="format-title">
  <div class="lib-wrap">
    <div class="section-label">Skill Format</div>
    <h2 id="format-title">Markdown with YAML frontmatter</h2>
    <p>Skills are plain Markdown files with structured frontmatter. The BM25 index operates at section and sub-section level, so well-structured skills return more precise search results.</p>
    <pre class="lib-pre"><code><span class="code-meta">---</span>
<span class="code-key">name</span>: <span class="code-str">my-skill</span>
<span class="code-key">version</span>: <span class="code-str">1.0.0</span>
<span class="code-key">category</span>: <span class="code-str">[development, patterns]</span>
<span class="code-key">description</span>: <span class="code-str">Brief description for search results</span>
<span class="code-meta">---</span>

<span class="code-cmd">## Section Heading</span>
<span class="code-comment">Content indexed at section level.</span>

<span class="code-cmd">### Sub-Section</span>
<span class="code-comment">Finer-grained content loaded independently via load_section.</span></code></pre>
    <p class="lib-install-note" style="margin-top: 16px;">Sections map directly to <code>load_section</code> calls. The BM25 index scores each chunk independently, so agents get the narrowest useful slice of knowledge rather than an entire document.</p>
  </div>
</section>

