<!-- LOCAL MODELS -->
<section class="lib-section" aria-labelledby="local-title">
  <div class="lib-wrap">
    <div class="section-label">Local Models</div>
    <h2 id="local-title">Model-agnostic</h2>
    <p>mcp-librarian speaks the MCP protocol over stdio, so the model on the other side does not matter to the server. Cloud or local, large or small.</p>
    <div class="lib-features-grid" role="list" style="margin-top: 24px;">
      <div class="lib-feature-card" role="listitem">
        <h3>Cloud Clients</h3>
        <p>Claude Code, Cursor, Windsurf, and other MCP-compatible coding agents. These are the clients I have tested against.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="lib-feature-card" role="listitem">
        <h3>Local Models</h3>
        <p>Ollama, LM Studio, llama.cpp, or a local model behind an MCP-compatible client. Search, load, install, and browse behave the same way. Skill writing quality tracks the model doing the writing.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p style="margin-top: 16px; font-size: 12px; color: var(--text-3); line-height: 1.6;">All 14 tools are available regardless of the model. The quality-dependent part is skill creation from research -- larger models tend to produce better-structured skills from the same web sources. Searching, loading, installing packs, and browsing are handled by the server, so they do not depend on the model.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- SECURITY -->
<section class="lib-section" aria-labelledby="security-title">
  <div class="lib-wrap">
    <div class="section-label">Security</div>
    <h2 id="security-title">Security model</h2>
    <p>mcp-librarian is written for the threat model of an AI agent with file system access, and assumes the agent can be manipulated through prompt injection in the knowledge base itself. These are pattern- and policy-based checks, not proofs: they will miss things and will occasionally flag content that is fine. No third-party audit has been done.</p>
    <ul class="lib-security-list" role="list">
      <li>
        <span class="lib-security-icon" aria-hidden="true">&#10003;</span>
        <span class="lib-security-text"><strong>Ed25519 signing</strong> -- skills are hashed and signed on write, and the signature is checked on every load. A file modified after signing fails the check and is flagged before its content reaches the agent.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="lib-security-icon" aria-hidden="true">&#10003;</span>
        <span class="lib-security-text"><strong>Content guard</strong> -- looks for known prompt injection patterns in skill content and rejects matches at write time. Instruction-like text inside fenced code blocks is allowed, so security examples still work. Pattern matching means both misses and false positives.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="lib-security-icon" aria-hidden="true">&#10003;</span>
        <span class="lib-security-text"><strong>Path guard</strong> -- all file operations are constrained to <code>MCP_LIBRARIAN_HOME</code>. Directory traversal attempts (<code>../</code>, absolute paths, symlink escapes) are rejected before any I/O occurs.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="lib-security-icon" aria-hidden="true">&#10003;</span>
        <span class="lib-security-text"><strong>Small network surface</strong> -- the server reaches the network only for tool calls that ask it to, such as <code>install_pack</code>, <code>research_topic</code>, and <code>fetch_page</code>. There is no telemetry, no version check, and no background connection.</span>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>
